{
  "dom_id": "step-e9981",
  "name": "Example: Identifying the Degree of a Product of Polynomials",
  "problem": "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an $x^8.$\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q,$ the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing $x^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
